## Target application stack for product specs

When agents design or implement an application described by a feature spec, the stack is fixed:

- **React** — UI. Components are declared as `FC<Props>` (see `61-react-component-signature`).
- **Nest SSR** — one NestJS process serves HTTP API and the first HTML for each page via server-side React (`renderToPipeableStream`). The client hydrates that HTML.
- **PostgreSQL** — the only database for those products. Only Nest accesses it, through TypeORM (`@nestjs/typeorm`, `pg` driver). Schema changes use TypeORM migrations. React never connects to the database.

That PostgreSQL stack applies to product specifications. It does not add a database to this visualizer.

The operation contract lives in Nest: controller, DTO, service. OpenAPI, when needed, comes from `@nestjs/swagger` on those DTOs. The React client calls that controller directly; there is no generated OpenAPI client as the type source of truth.

## Parent rules do not apply here

Rules under `Projects/.cursor/rules` describe a different product: legacy ERMO/Omni admin UI patterns (dictionary tables, design-system kits, client-side store libraries, webpack micro-frontends, and OpenAPI-to-client codegen). **That parent stack does not apply to tasks in this repository.** For agent behavior and application design tied to specs here, this file and `.cursor/plans/react-nest-ssr-postgres/SPEC.md` (when present) are the stack source of truth.

Prefer robust, validated work over fast work. If a requirement is unclear, stop and ask the user before guessing.
